    {
      "column_index": 5,
      "header_id": "C40",
      "x_range": [
        7018.6,
        null
      ],
      "section_title": "Orienting & Flattening (for Digital Fabrication)",
      "section_number": 5,
      "teaching_text": "5- Orienting & Flattening (for Digital Fabrication): \r\n\r\n- Build a new Surface in Rhino and give it a sharp curvature by manipulating the control points.\r\n\r\n- Use the \"Mesh Surface\" component.\r\n\r\n- Triangulate all meshes.\r\n\r\n- Convert all meshes into Surfaces.\r\n\r\n- Make a \"Square Grid\" in order to use the grid cells as targets for the oriented and flatenned surfaces.\r\n\r\n- We will check several ways of orienting and flatenning surfaces in 3D, from being placed on a geometry to being placed flat on XY plane.",
      "teaching_panel_id": "C39",
      "additional_teaching_panels": [],
      "sub_annotations": [
        {
          "id": "C129",
          "text": "When we want to manipulate the Mesh Faces, the \"Deconstruct Mesh\" component is not useful to choose the Faces >> List item does NOT work!\r\n\r\nHowever, we can use \"Face Boundaries\" component and then convert them to NURBS by using \"Boundary Surfaces\"! We are now able to choose the Faces!",
          "topic": "When we want to manipulate the Mesh Faces, the \"Deconstruct Mesh\" component is not useful to choose the Faces >> List item does NOT work!",
          "pos": [
            7022.1,
            720.4
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "C137",
          "text": "We also sort the planes so that they are in the same order as their respective surfaces",
          "topic": "We also sort the planes so that they are in the same order as their respective surfaces",
          "pos": [
            8703.8,
            1066.5
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "C190",
          "text": "1- Orienting:\r\n- Use \"Orient\" component.\r\n- Feed the new Surfaces into the G INPUT.\r\n- Evaluate all surfaces and use the Frames OUTPUT from the Evaluate Surface as INPUT A in the \"Orient\" component.\r\n- Get the center of all grid cells from the Square Grid andfeed them in the INPUT B of \"Orient\" as the target planes.\r\n- Result: All surfaces are oriented onto the XY Plane and placed inside the Square Grid.\r\nHowever, the oriented surfaces are not ordered according to their sizes, and it looks like all cells have been filled, while there are less surfaces on the original geometry than the number of cells.\r\nSo, let's try to solve these issues...",
          "topic": "Orienting",
          "pos": [
            8162.6,
            338.4
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "C191",
          "text": "2- Sorting:\r\n- Sort all surfaces according to their areas.",
          "topic": "Sorting",
          "pos": [
            8686,
            338.4
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "C192",
          "text": "3- Short Listing:\r\n- Use the \"Short List\" component to get rid of additional cells from the Square Grid by using the shortest list length.",
          "topic": "Short Listing",
          "pos": [
            9209.8,
            338.4
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "C193",
          "text": "4- Orient Direction:\r\n- Try the \"Orient Direction\" component and see the difference.\r\n- It looks like all surfaces are rotated randomly. Although this did not happen with the previously used components, let us try to solve this issue.",
          "topic": "Orient Direction",
          "pos": [
            9729.4,
            338.4
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "C194",
          "text": "5- Orienting each surface alone by its geoemtric properties: Longest side to be along X axis:\r\n- Get the Edges of the Geometry from previous component \"Orient Direction\" using the \"Brep Edges\" component.\r\n- Get the Length of all curves using the \"Curve Length\" component.\r\n- Sort all Curve Lengths using the \"Sort List\" component.\r\n- Reverse the A OUTPUT (so that the List starts with the greatest values of Curve Lenghts).\r\n- Extract the Start & End Points of each longest edge using the \"End Points\" component.\r\n- Construct 2Point Vectors based on the direction of Curves: From Start Point to End Point Direction.\r\n- Extract the Middle Point of each Greatest Curve so that it can be used as the Reference Point when Orienting again.\r\n- Prepare Target Direction Vectors (in X Direction) at the Centroids of the Square Grid Cells.\r\n- IMPORTANT: Match the new Vectors Lengths at the Centroids of the Square Grid with the Vectors made by the Curves: Use an \"Amplitude\" component.",
          "topic": "Orienting each surface alone by its geoemtric properties: Longest side to be along X axis",
          "pos": [
            10315,
            338.4
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "C195",
          "text": "Assignment 7c:\r\n\r\n- Build a new Surface in Rhino and repeat the same procedure.",
          "topic": "Assignment 7c",
          "pos": [
            11720,
            338.4
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "C236",
          "text": "Alternative method:\r\nOrienting surfaces, evaluating the oriented surfaces and using their respective frames",
          "topic": "Alternative method",
          "pos": [
            8881.1,
            1302.1
          ]
        }
      ],
